<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yemelyan Pugachev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1742–<a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yemelyan Pugachev was a Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> who in 1773 claimed to be the murdered Emperor Peter III and led the largest Cossack and peasant rising in Russian history against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. Backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Yaik</a> Cossacks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>, factory serfs and peasants, his forces burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and swept through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region. Defeated in 1774, he was betrayed by his own followers and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in January 1775.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yemelyan-pugachev.jpg" alt="Yemelyan Pugachev" width="299" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yemelyan_Pugachev_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pugachev was the youngest son of a small Don Cossack landowner and was born in the stanitsa of Zimoveyskaya, in present-day Volgograd Oblast. He enlisted at 17, married a Cossack girl, Sofya Nedyuzheva, within the year, and joined the Russian army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. He returned home in 1762 and reached the Cossack rank of khorunzhiy. At the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Bender</a> in 1770, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a>, he first showed a flair for impersonation by telling comrades that his sword was a gift from his godfather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-i-grand-duke-of-oldenburg">Peter I</a>.</p><p>In 1770 he left the army and became a fugitive. He was arrested and escaped several times, spent time among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> of the Terek, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the autumn of 1772 with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a>. He settled near present-day Volsk and probably heard there of the rebellion of the Yaik Cossacks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The idea of posing as the dead Emperor Peter III came to Pugachev early. Peter III had been overthrown and murdered in 1762 by supporters of his wife, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>. Pugachev promised to restore privileges to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and the Old Belief, and in 1773 he began an open revolt, proclaiming an end to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. His army overran much of the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Urals, and he gained the support of industrial peasants in the Urals. He set up a College of War with four judges to organise his army, and it paid wages and bounties to the Cossacks. His chief success was the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1774, where the city was burned to the ground.</p><p>General Pyotr Panin was slow to act against him, because of transport difficulties and indiscipline among his poorly paid soldiers. In August 1774 General Michelsohn crushed the rebels near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Tsaritsyn</a>, and about ten thousand were killed or taken prisoner. Panin carried out savage reprisals after the capture of Penza. On 14 September 1774 Pugachev's own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> handed him over at Yaitsk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Alexander Suvorov</a> had him placed in an iron cage and sent to Simbirsk and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. He was executed publicly in Bolotnaya Square on 21 January 1775, beheaded and then quartered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> had been trying to reform the provincial administration, but the horrors of the revolt made her drop other reforms, in particular attempts to emancipate the serfs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a> wrote a history of the rebellion and used it in his novel The Captain's Daughter (1836).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yemelyan Pugachev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government remembered the risings of Stenka Razin and <b>Yemelyan Pugachev</b> and kept the hosts loyal with land and privileges.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Russian state tightened control, discontent grew; in 1772 a revolt broke out, and in 1773 Yaik Cossacks backed <b>Yemelyan Pugachev's</b> rebellion against Catherine the Great.</blockquote><a href="/state/ural-cossack-host">Ural Cossack Host</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pugachyovskaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.01,43.16&amp;year=1763" aria-label="Pugachyovskaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5555 483 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yemelyan Pugachev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6005 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6005 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.01,43.16&amp;year=1763">What was here in 1763? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
